*
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    APPEND.SD.PATH ON | OFF
*
*    Says whether SD's program directory is on the Windows system PATH, so
*    that "sd" runs from any directory instead of only from its own.
*
*       ON   put it on the system PATH
*       OFF  take it off
*
*    With no keyword it reports the current setting and changes nothing.
*
* ***WHY THIS VERB EXISTS, AND IT IS NOT SYMMETRY WITH THE OTHERS.***  The
* installer's tickbox could set this once and never again: nothing removed the
* entry except the uninstaller, so clearing the box on a later run did nothing
* at all (PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 89, defect B).  The owner then ruled that an upgrade
* shows no tasks page whatsoever - "if the admin wants to make additional
* choices, we have given them the command line tools" - and found the gap in his
* own ruling the same day: "we do not have a user command to change the path."
* This is that command.  Without it the ruling would leave the setting with no
* way to be changed after the first install.
*
* ***THE NAME AVOIDS A REAL COLLISION AND WAS CHOSEN FOR IT.***  Inside SD
* "path" already means the ACCOUNT's path - pathname is a VOC keyword, WHERE
* prints "account pathname", @PATH holds it - so SET.PATH would read as setting
* that.  OS.PATH collides with VALID_OS_PATH, which means a host filesystem
* path.  APPEND.SD.PATH cannot be read as either.
*
* ***IT IS THE SYSTEM PATH, NOT THE USER'S.***  The value lives in HKLM, under
* Session Manager\Environment, so the change applies to every account on the
* machine - which is what the installer always did, and is why this is
* administrator-only.
*
* ***THE WORK IS IN sd-path.ps1 AND DELIBERATELY NOT IN HERE.***  Same reason
* api-listener.ps1 records: a script can be parse-checked, byte-checked and
* unit-tested on its own, and BASIC embedded in a verb cannot.  Editing PATH is
* the one setting on this machine that breaks everything if it is got wrong, so
* test-sdpath-units.ps1 drives its logic against synthetic values - 24 checks,
* including the accumulated-empties case of 16 Aug 2026 - rather than
* rehearsing on the real one.
*
* ***NO CONFIRMATION ON OFF, UNLIKE SSH.SERVER REMOVE.***  That verb asks
* because removing the server strands every SD account with no way in.  This
* one takes away a convenience: "sd" still runs, from its own directory or by
* full path, and APPEND.SD.PATH ON puts it straight back.  A prompt standing in
* front of a reversible change teaches people to answer prompts without reading
* them.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal

program $append.sd.path
$catalog $APNDPATH

* int$keys.h carries K$ADMINISTRATOR and K$WINPATH, and is not optional: both
* are defined there and in neither of the others.  Leaving it out compiles to
* an unknown-symbol failure that costs a whole cycle to discover.
$include keys.h
$include int$keys.h
$include parser.h
$include err.h

*  THE REPORTING FORM, PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 90.  ps_script returns a status and
*  throws the script's output away, so the -Show path printed NOTHING - this
*  verb is administrator-gated, so it always reaches the elevated helper and
*  never the fall-through that would have reached the terminal.  Measured on
*  the installed tree 31 Aug 2026, and remote.api does the same.
   deffun ps_script_out(script, want.out) calling '!ps_script_out'

   prompt ''

*  ADMINISTRATOR ONLY, THE SAME GATE REMOTE.API AND SSH.SERVER USE.  The system
*  PATH is machine-wide; it is not an ordinary user's to change.
   if not(kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1)) then
      stop sysmsg(2001)  ;* Command requires administrator privileges
   end

*  ASSIGNED HERE FOR BCOMP, NOT FOR THE LOGIC.  bootstrap.py fails the WHOLE
*  bootstrap on an "is not assigned a value" warning, and BCOMP reads the source
*  in order.  Every value is also the safe one: no action, no change.
   action = ''
   arg    = ''
   rc     = 0
   report = ''
   both   = ''

   parser = "!PARSER"
   call @parser(PARSER$RESET, 0, @sentence, 0)
   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, token, keyword)   ;* Verb append.sd.path
   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, action, keyword)  ;* ON | OFF

   action = upcase(trim(action))

*  NO KEYWORD IS A REPORT, NOT AN ERROR.  Asking what the setting is should
*  never be able to change it, so this runs -Show and stops before anything
*  else.  -Show is also the one mode that needs no elevation.
   if token.type = PARSER$END or action = '' then
      arg = ' -Show'
      gosub run.report
      if report # '' then
         crt report
      end
      if rc # 0 then
         crt sysmsg(10153, 'SHOW', rc)  ;* Could not read the system PATH
      end
      stop
   end

   begin case
      case action = 'ON'
         arg = ' -Add'
      case action = 'OFF'
         arg = ' -Remove'
      case @true
         stop sysmsg(10152)  ;* append.sd.path on | off
   end case

*  ***CAPTURED EVEN THOUGH THE SUCCESS PATH PRINTS NONE OF IT.***  sd-path.ps1
*  says WHY when it declines, and message 10155 below promises the reader those
*  lines - a promise that was false until PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 90, because the
*  elevated helper threw the output away.  Captured here and shown only when
*  something went wrong, so a successful "on" stays one line.
   gosub run.report

   if rc # 0 and report # '' then
      crt report
   end

*  ***EXIT 2 IS A REFUSAL, NOT A FAILURE, AND THE SCRIPT HAS ALREADY SAID WHY.***
*  sd-path.ps1 exits 2 when it declines to act - the directory does not exist,
*  the system PATH came back empty, or its own guard found the rebuilt value was
*  not what was intended.  In every one of those it printed the reason and wrote
*  nothing.  Restating it here would either duplicate the script or, worse,
*  paraphrase it wrongly, so this points at what is now genuinely on screen.
   if rc = 2 then
      stop sysmsg(10155)  ;* Nothing was changed - the reason is above
   end

   if rc # 0 then
      stop sysmsg(10153, action, rc)  ;* Could not change the system PATH
   end

*  ***THE SCRIPT ALREADY SAID "OPEN A NEW TERMINAL", SO THIS DOES NOT.***  An
*  existing shell keeps the PATH it started with, and sd-path.ps1 prints that on
*  both the add and the remove path.
   if action = 'ON' then
      crt sysmsg(10154)  ;* SD's program directory is on the system PATH
   end else
      crt sysmsg(10156)  ;* SD's program directory is off the system PATH
   end

   stop

* ===========================================================================

*  ***ONE ENTRY POINT, AND IT ALWAYS CAPTURES.***  There was a second that
*  called ps_script and discarded the output; both paths want the output now -
*  the report to print it, the action to explain a refusal - so it was removed
*  rather than left to read as a supported alternative.
*
*  Field 1 is the status ps_script would have returned; fields 2 onwards are
*  what the script printed.
run.report:
   ps  =       '& ' : char(39) : kernel(K$WINPATH, '/sd-path.ps1') : char(39) : arg
   ps := @fm : 'exit $LASTEXITCODE'
   both   = ps_script_out(ps, @true)
   rc     = field(both, @fm, 1)
   report = field(both, @fm, 2, 9999)
   return

end

* END-CODE
